meta {
  name: 9 Record consent
  type: http
  seq: 9
}
put {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/customers/{{customerEmail}}/consent
  body: json
}
body:json {
  {
    "acceptedTerms": true,
    "acceptedPrivacy": true
  }
}
docs {
  Acceptance of the Terms and Conditions and the Privacy Policy, recorded as two timestamps on the profile
  (`termsAcceptedAt`, `privacyAcceptedAt`).

  ⚠️ **Two booleans, not two dates — the server stamps the time.** A consent record whose timestamp the
  consenting party supplies proves nothing, and a device with a wrong clock would file the acceptance in the
  wrong year.

  ⚠️ **Both flags are always written, so `false` means "not accepted" rather than "unchanged".** Send
  `{"acceptedTerms": false, "acceptedPrivacy": false}` and both timestamps come back `null`: withdrawing
  consent is a state the record has to be able to express, and a partial-update shape would make an unticked
  box silently keep the previous acceptance.

  A dedicated route, targeted `$set` on those two fields only — the name, the phone number and the
  subscription list all survive it.

  Answers:
  - `200` with the updated customer on success.
  - `403` for another account's email. Ownership-guarded.
  - `404` when no customer has that email.

  Verify with `2 Get customer by email`.
}
